## Active-Active vs Active-Passive Setup

### This Infrastructure: Active-Active

**Active-Active Setup**:
- **Definition**: All servers actively handle traffic simultaneously
- **In this setup**: Both Server 1 and Server 2 receive and process requests concurrently
- **Advantages**:
  - Better resource utilization (all servers work)
  - Higher total capacity (combined capacity of all servers)
  - Better performance under normal conditions
- **Disadvantages**:
  - More complex configuration
  - Requires session synchronization for stateful applications
  - Higher infrastructure costs (all servers must be adequately provisioned)

### Alternative: Active-Passive Setup

**Active-Passive Setup**:
- **Definition**: Only one server (active) handles traffic; others (passive) remain on standby
- **Behavior**: Passive servers only activate when the active server fails
- **Advantages**:
  - Simpler configuration
  - Clear failover mechanism
  - Lower operational complexity
- **Disadvantages**:
  - Wasted resources (passive servers idle)
  - Lower total capacity
  - Failover delay when switching servers

